/* Beat 7 - the payoff, composed onto ScreenScaffold and brought to
 * `Splice Reveal.dc.html` in Phase 9 Task 16. The hybrid is the warm thing
 * on a recessive ground (bible 10.1), so this is the one screen that centres
 * and enlarges.
 *
 * WHAT THIS SHEET IS FOR: geometry the handoff gives THIS screen, the
 * arrangement of the components in it, and the two overrides `HeroBand`'s
 * own comment says a band screen is expected to make. Nothing here restates
 * a `.panel-*`, a `.t-*`, an `.elev-*` or a component's internals.
 *
 * WHAT LEFT IN PHASE 8 TASK 10 AND HAS NOT COME BACK: the root's gutter (the
 * scaffold's content region), `__headline`'s type (`.t-hero`),
 * `__child-line`'s and `__consumption`'s colour and size (`.t-secondary`),
 * and `__next`'s whole treatment (`.btn-primary`).
 *
 * WHAT LEFT IN THIS TASK: `.splice-reveal-view__child`'s `.elev-2` is
 * `HeroBand`'s own now - the band adds it - so the one declaration this file
 * used to carry for the hero card (a margin) went with the hand-rolled
 * wrapper. The consumption line's panel fill is `.panel-amber`/
 * `.panel-violet`, switched in `Bind` rather than declared here.
 */
.splice-reveal-view { flex-grow: 1; }

/* THE CENTRING IS ON THE SCAFFOLD'S CONTENT CONTAINER, which is the element
 * that actually holds these children - the ScrollView routes them into
 * `unity-scroll-view__content-container`, so `align-items` on any ancestor
 * above it does nothing. Reaching a descendant from a screen's own sheet is
 * the legal direction (CampaignSelectView.uss's note): a COMPONENT's sheet
 * cannot style the container it was put in, which is upward.
 *
 * `stretch` FOR THE TWO FULL-WIDTH SURFACES, AND THAT IS WHY THIS IS NOT
 * `center` ANY MORE. Through Phase 8 every child of this column was
 * content-sized and centring them was the whole layout. The band and the
 * traits card are full-bleed in the handoff (`:43` and `:71` are both
 * `margin: <n>px 12px` on a 430 frame), and a centred flex child shrinks to
 * its content - so `center` here would have drawn a 372px band around
 * whatever width its subject came to. The headline centres its own text
 * instead, one rule down. */
.splice-reveal-view .unity-scroll-view__content-container { align-items: stretch; }

/* A mutation is the rare outcome and the reason the screen exists. Violet is
 * the brand colour and this is where it earns the emphasis. Two classes, so
 * it outranks `.t-hero`'s --ink. */
.splice-reveal-view.mutated .splice-reveal-view__headline { color: var(--violet-text); }

/* `:40` - `text-align: center; padding: 10px 24px 0` over a 26px Baloo
 * headline. Ours is `.t-hero`'s 28px, two over, which is the nearest thing
 * on the scale; --text-card-hero (26) is exact but is Task 14b's founder
 * CARD heading and this is a page-level line. */
.splice-reveal-view__headline {
    white-space: normal;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------- the hero band */

/* THE BAND IS `HeroBand` AND IT NEEDS NO RADIUS OVERRIDE, which is the one
 * instance of six that can say so: `Splice Reveal.dc.html:43` is 26 and
 * `--radius-band` IS 26. Its height comes from `HeroBand.Fix(372)` in C#,
 * where both halves of the fix (the surface, never the elevation wrapper)
 * are written from one method - see that component's class comment for the
 * 160px of shadow-around-bare-paper that rule exists to prevent.
 *
 * NO MARGIN HERE EITHER. `.elev-2` already pads by --elev-2-spread so the
 * drop has somewhere to fall, and the card below it carries its own
 * `margin-bottom` from `SectionCard`. */

/* `:45` is `r 140` - a 280px ring against `--band-ring`'s 264 - and `:46` is
 * `r 106`, a 212px pool against the component's 196. Both are stretched
 * textures, so a different diameter is the same 52 dashes at a different
 * scale (generate-textures.py's band-ring block has the arithmetic and says
 * so in as many words). `Tokens.uss`'s own note names 280 as one of the two
 * band rings that are NOT the token, which is exactly this. 106 is half of
 * 212. */
.splice-reveal-view__child .hero-band__ring { width: 280px; height: 280px; }
.splice-reveal-view__child .hero-band__glow {
    width: 212px;
    height: 212px;
    border-radius: 106px;
}

/* `:57` - the child is drawn at 186x186 inside the 212px pool, leaving 13px
 * of violet visible on each side. `HeroBand.uss` sizes a HALOED subject's
 * slot to 160 by default and says what is left: "Remaining room on the 61%
 * is HeroSlot's disc size, traded against pool visibility." This screen has
 * the handoff's own number for that trade, so it takes it: 186 of 280 is
 * 66%, against the component's 160-of-264 61%.
 *
 * THE SLOT'S OWN RING IS ALREADY HIDDEN by `.hero-band__subject--haloed` -
 * the band's ring replaces it - so only the slot and its disc move. 93 is
 * half of 186. Three rules, the same three `HeroBand.uss` writes, because
 * `--hero-slot` and `.hero-slot__disc` are both flat numbers in the
 * component's sheet and moving one without the other overflows the disc. */
.splice-reveal-view__child .hero-band__subject--haloed .hero-slot {
    width: 186px;
    height: 186px;
}
.splice-reveal-view__child .hero-band__subject--haloed .hero-slot__disc {
    width: 186px;
    height: 186px;
    border-radius: 93px;
    /* AND IT IS TRANSPARENT, BECAUSE THE POOL IS ALREADY THE BACKDROP.
     * `Splice Reveal.dc.html:57-58` draws the child as a bare 186px art slot
     * with NOTHING behind it - `:46`'s `r 106` violet circle IS what it sits
     * on - where `HeroSlot`'s disc is a species tint sized for a slot
     * standing alone on paper. Left opaque, a 186px teal circle covered all
     * but 13px of the pool and put Vetch's own colour where the screen's
     * brand violet belongs; it is in the first capture of this screen.
     * `HeroSlot.uss` makes exactly this call one level down, for exactly
     * this reason, when a `CreatureStage` is placed in the disc. rgba rather
     * than a hex so verify-uss-tokens.sh's check 1 sees a colour that is not
     * a colour. */
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
}

.splice-reveal-view__child-slot { align-items: center; }

/* `:60` - `display: inline-flex; border-radius: 999px; padding: 6px 13px;
 * margin-top: 14px`, a 12px sparkle beside an 11px uppercase label.
 *
 * 14px IS HALF THE MEASURED HEIGHT AND NOT `--radius-pill`, which does not
 * exist and must not: 6 + 6 of padding around an 11px line box (~16 at this
 * face's line height) is ~28, and half of 28 is 14. That coincides with
 * --radius-row and is not it - a row radius that moved would take this pill
 * with it for no reason the two share.
 *
 * `.panel-amber` BRINGS THE FILL AND THE PADDING; this overrides the radius
 * and the horizontal padding and adds the ramp and the ring. THE RAMP AND
 * THE RING LOOK LIKE `MutationBanner`'s AND ARE NOT A RESTATEMENT OF IT:
 * that component is a full-width strip with a 24px white disc in it, its
 * rules are all scoped to `.mutation-banner`, and it cannot be a centred
 * pill. What is shared is the ASSET - amber-ramp.png, the handoff's own
 * `linear-gradient(100deg, #fdf0d0, #f9e0a8)` - which is a texture two
 * places may read, and --amber for the handoff's `rgba(196,150,45,.3)`
 * inset ring, which USS cannot express as a shadow and draws as a border
 * (`.option--selected` makes the same substitution).
 *
 * `align-self: center` because the column is `stretch` now; the pill is the
 * one child in the band that is content-width. */
.splice-reveal-view__pill {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    align-self: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    border-radius: 14px;
    padding-left: 13px;
    padding-right: 13px;
    padding-top: 6px;
    padding-bottom: 6px;
    margin-top: 14px;
    background-image: url("project:///Assets/UI/Art/amber-ramp.png");
    -unity-background-scale-mode: stretch-to-fill;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: var(--amber);
}

/* TWO CLASSES, to beat icons.uss's 19px and --mute-soft without depending on
 * sheet order. The handoff draws this mark at 12px; --amber-text is the ink
 * `MutationBanner` measured to 6.30:1 on white and is already this project's
 * general amber, not a warning-only brown. */
.splice-reveal-view .splice-reveal-view__pill-spark {
    width: 12px;
    height: 12px;
    margin-right: 7px;
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: var(--amber-text);
}

/* `:62` - 11px, 800, `letter-spacing: .06em`. `.t-micro` is 10px with the
 * tracking and `.t-warn` is --amber-text; only the size is restated, and up
 * rather than down, so bible 10.6's floor is untouched. */
.splice-reveal-view .splice-reveal-view__pill-text { font-size: var(--text-secondary); }

/* `:64` - 30px Baloo, 8px under the pill. `.t-hero` is 28. `:65` is 11.5px
 * muted, 1px under it. */
.splice-reveal-view__child-name {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    white-space: normal;
}
.splice-reveal-view__child-line {
    margin-top: 1px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    white-space: normal;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------- the traits card */

/* The handoff's two-sided card header - `:72-75`. Same construction and same
 * reason as the chamber's; `SpliceChamberView.CardHeader` carries the note,
 * including why this is `center` rather than the handoff's `baseline`. */
.splice-reveal-view__card-header {
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
}
.splice-reveal-view__note { flex-shrink: 0; }

.splice-reveal-view__traits { margin-top: 10px; }

/* `:77` - `display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 9px`.
 *
 * THE HAIRLINE BETWEEN ROWS IS AN ELEMENT AND NOT A BORDER, because USS on
 * 6000.6.0f1 HAS NO STRUCTURAL PSEUDO-CLASSES - `:first-child` and
 * `:not(...)` are not in its grammar, so "a top border on every row but the
 * first" cannot be written and a rule that tried would be dropped or refused
 * at import. `Bind` inserts Theme's own `.divider` between the rows instead,
 * which is `height: 1px; background: var(--divider); margin: 8px 0` - the
 * handoff's `:82` declaration property for property. */
.splice-reveal-view__trait-row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
}

/* `:80` - 10.5px, 800, tinted per source. The tint is the trait's owner's
 * ink in the handoff and --mute here: the chip beside it already carries the
 * species colour, and a second tinted thing on the same row would make the
 * row read as two chips. Named in the report. */
.splice-reveal-view__trait-source { flex-shrink: 0; margin-left: var(--space-2); }

/* `:95` - the note strip under the rows. The FILL and the RADIUS come from
 * `.panel-amber`/`.panel-violet`, switched in `Bind`; this is the gap above
 * it and the wrap a sentence needs. */
.splice-reveal-view__consumption { white-space: normal; margin-top: 11px; }

/* --------------------------------------------------------- the two parents */

/* The two consumed parents, still whole. bible 2.1 makes this the FTUE's
 * whole lesson, so they are shown at full strength and the consumption line
 * in the card above carries the fact instead of the parents being greyed
 * out. NOT IN THE HANDOFF AT ALL - this view's class comment has why the
 * spec wins over the mock here. */
.splice-reveal-view__parents {
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
}

.splice-reveal-view__parent { margin-left: var(--space-1); margin-right: var(--space-1); }

/* AND THE PARENT TILES DROP THEIR TRAIT CHIPS ON THIS SCREEN ONLY.
 *
 * splice_confirm_spec 3's rule about repetition is the reason, not the
 * height: the card directly above these two tiles states every trait the
 * child holds AND names which parent brought it, by name - so a second,
 * fuller copy of each parent's trait list 40px below it is the "three
 * different phrasings of the same fact" that section warns reads as evasion.
 * What this row is for is the other half of bible 2.1's lesson: THESE TWO
 * CREATURES ARE GONE. The picture, the name and the generation say that.
 *
 * IT ALSO COSTS 90px, WHICH IS WHY THE CAPTURE IS WHAT FOUND IT. Two chips
 * wrap to two rows in a 160px tile and each chip row renders ~39px tall, so
 * the pair pushed this row - the one the handoff does not draw at all - a
 * further 90px below a fold it was already under. The residual that remains
 * is named in the task report.
 *
 * A SCREEN'S SHEET REACHING A COMPONENT'S DESCENDANT IS THE LEGAL DIRECTION
 * (StatCell.uss's closing note); `display: none` rather than a `Bind`
 * parameter because `CreatureCard` is shared by five screens and none of the
 * other four wants this. */
.splice-reveal-view__parent .creature-card__traits { display: none; }
